WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.520
Singapore recorded the world's highest air pollution and activated medical subsidies for haze-related illness. This is Omni View, episode 203. Swiss Monitor IQ Air published the Monday figures as smoke kept drifting in from fires on Borneo and Sumatra, the South China Morning Post reports. The paper calls Singapore's response a practical public health step for rising illness among residents. It also argues that national tools hit a hard limit when the blazes sit in Indonesia.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.620
Asia, a burden Malaysia shares.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.300
The al-Nenio climate pattern has made those island fires worse.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.300
Government programs now offer more affordable care for people with smoke-related breathing

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:40.900
problems.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.200
The same plume has closed schools in parts of Malaysia.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.860
Malaysian authorities have concentrated on those immediate steps, including shutting classrooms.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.740
Singapore has put the emphasis on access to treatment instead.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.280
The two governments still share an interest in managing the health effects in front of them.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.900
The fires themselves have not gone out.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.960
Officials will keep watching air quality readings and how many people use the treatment programs.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.060
Hurricane Simon tracked parallel to Mexico's Pacific Coast on Sunday as a category 3 storm.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:13.520
Deutsche Wella reports.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.380
That coverage stresses the danger for tourist resorts and for inland communities whose

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.280
housing is less able to take a beating.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.900
Heavy rain and strong winds reach towns in Chalisco and Colima states.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:25.100
States.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.640
Mexican authorities placed 28 municipalities under the highest cyclone alert.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.500
They placed another 93 under high alert.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.920
The U.S. National Hurricane Center warned of life-threatening flooding, mudslides, and storm

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:38.960
surge near the coast.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.860
Local residents described flooded homes and a reluctance to leave.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.800
Civil protection bulletins focused on alert levels and expected rainfall totals.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.560
Authorities have been balancing the need to evacuate against the practical difficulty of

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.540
moving people out of higher risk ground. Forecasters expect the storm to lose strength

